// ⚠ THIS FIXTURE IS A COST SENSOR FOR THE ELISION ENGINE, and it exists
// because no population the engine was priced over could see the program it
// pins. Measured 2026-08-31l on ONE binary, one variable apart: this compiles
// unarmed (rc 0) and is REFUSED by `ltmintinst`, `ltmintmeetrg` and
// `ltmintmeetamb` alike —
//   call to 'swap_ref' arg 2: variance mismatch — expected &mut &mut i64,
//   got &mut &mut i64
// — two regions the compiler minted, printed alike because nobody wrote them.
//
// WHY IT ESCAPED. `swap_ref<T>(a:&mut T, b:&mut T)` binds T from argument 1,
// minted regions and all, and argument 2 is then compared against the BOUND T.
// That is first-occurrence-wins at a TYPE parameter — the same defect the
// struct-literal MEET closed for lifetime binders, one binder-kind over — and
// the meet cannot be asked here: T occurs under `&mut`, so the demand is
// EQUALITY, not "a region both outlive". Rust accepts it because both regions
// are inference VARIABLES and get equated; ours are two fixed minted names.
// ⇒ what remains after the meet is UNIFICATION of minted regions, not a
// lattice meet and NOT CFG points. PROBES.md 2026-08-31l.
//
// THE PAIR, one construct apart: tests/imported/fail/borrowck/
// borrowck-swap-mut-base-ptr.logos is this program plus a live `&*t0` across
// the swap. It is ILLEGAL (upstream E0502) and must stay refused. This one is
// legal and must stay compiled. The non-generic form of the same swap
// (`fn both(a:&mut &mut i64, b:&mut &mut i64)`) is NOT refused by any arm —
// measured — so the class is the GENERIC binding site, not `&mut` itself.
package bc_ltmintgen_two_minted_regions_one_typaram;

use logos.lang.mem;

fn foo(a: &mut i64, b: &mut i64) -> i64 {
    let mut t0 = a;
    let mut t1 = b;
    swap_ref(&mut t0, &mut t1);
    *t1 = 22i64;
    return *t0;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut x: i64 = 1i64;
    let mut y: i64 = 2i64;
    if foo(&mut x, &mut y) != 2i64 { return 1i32; }
    if x != 22i64 { return 2i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
